(A thing is a value: every copy of it, by assignment, by a parameter or by
 a return, copies its list, map and buffer fields too, so changing one in
 the copy never changes the original, and the reverse.)

A thing called note has
  a list called items is ["eggs"],
  a map called counts is {"eggs": 6},
  a buffer called body is "milk".

A thing called folder has
  a note called front,
  a list called labels.

To 'show both' with a note called left and a note called right.
  Print left.
  Print right.

(Declared from another, then each changed.)
a note called original.
a note called duplicate is original.
append "duplicate only" to duplicate's items.
Set duplicate's counts's "eggs" to 12.
append "!" to duplicate's body.
append "original only" to original's items.
'show both' with original and duplicate.

(Assigned over an existing thing, by each spelling.)
a note called later.
Set later to original.
append "later only" to later's items.
the later is original.
Set original's counts's "later" to 0.
'show both' with original and later.

(Passed to a function, which changes its own copy.)
To 'scribble on' with a note called given.
  append "scribbled" to given's items.
  Set given's counts's "eggs" to 0.
  copy "ink" to given's body.
  Print given.

'scribble on' with original.
Print original.

(Returned from a function: the result is the caller's own.)
To 'a fresh note'.
  a note called made.
  append "made" to made's items.
  Return a note, made.

To 'the same note' with a note called given.
  Return a note, given.

a note called 'first made' is 'a fresh note'.
a note called 'second made' is 'a fresh note'.
append "second only" to 'second made''s items.
'show both' with 'first made' and 'second made'.
The returned is 'the same note' of original.
append "returned only" to returned's items.
'show both' with original and returned.

(A nested thing's collections are copied with the thing that holds it.)
a folder called drawer.
append "drawer" to drawer's labels.
a folder called spare is drawer.
append "spare" to spare's front's items.
append "spare" to spare's labels.
Set drawer's front's counts's "drawer" to 1.
Print drawer.
Print spare.
Set spare's front to drawer's front.
append "front copy" to spare's front's items.
Print drawer's front's items.
Print spare's front's items.

(The same holds for a thing that lives inside a function.)
To 'copy a local'.
  a note called here.
  a note called there is here.
  append "there" to there's items.
  Print here's items.
  Print there's items.

'copy a local'.
